# The snake tour: a serpentine -- four half-circles of alternating
# curvature, driven as arcs.
#
# THIS FILE WAS CALLED spline.tour AND WAS NOT A SPLINE. Renamed
# 2026-09-01 at the stakeholder's direction. A spline is a fitted
# curve followed with pure pursuit, which is what spline.tour now
# actually does; this is a chain of circular arcs and the name should
# say so.
#
# What it is still worth testing: repeated left/right curvature
# reversal. The curvature flips sign at every join with no straight in
# between to let the heading servo settle. circle.tour holds one
# curvature, infinity.tour reverses it twice per lap, this reverses it
# three times in a row -- and unlike spline.tour it does so at exactly
# constant radius, so a tracking error here is the drivetrain rather
# than the follower.
#
# SIZING (2026-09-01). r = 125 mm.
#
# THE ADVANCE AXIS IS PERPENDICULAR TO THE START HEADING. Starting at
# the origin facing +x and turning CCW, the first half circle ends at
# (0, +2r) facing -x: progress is SIDEWAYS. Four bends advance 8r along
# y and swing +-r in x, so the figure spans **250 x 1000 mm**. Against
# the working budget of +-550 x +-350 mm (tours/FIELD.md) it fits only
# with the advance along the field's LONG axis, so **stage the robot
# facing along the SHORT (N-S) axis**, at (0, -500) from the centre.
#
# r = 150 with four bends would advance 1200 mm and does not fit; five
# bends at any useful radius does not either.
#
#   v = omega * r, with omega = 114.6 deg/s = 2.0 rad/s
#   -> v = 2.0 * 125 = 250 mm/s.
#
# SEGMENT SIZE IS NOT FREE. moveX() splits any move with a nonzero
# distance and |rotation| >= 50 deg (kTurnFirstAngleRad) into a pivot
# THEN a straight. MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01
# (reports/tours-20260901/): circle.tour written as one 360 deg arc
# drove a 942 mm STRAIGHT LINE. Every arc here is 45 deg, so a half
# circle is four of them.

# PIVOT TUNING (MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01, interleaved A/B so battery
# drift hits both arms equally). Pivot cruise 188 mm/s (omega 180 deg/s)
# beats 300 mm/s (286 deg/s) decisively: excess +1.34 deg sd 0.35 versus
# +6.88 deg sd 1.03, n=8 each. pivot_overrun was then swept AT 188 mm/s
# and 0.68 -- the value the closure sweep found at 300 mm/s -- leaves
# +1.44 deg on the table; 2.0 is the zero crossing (1.8 -> +0.29,
# 2.2 -> -0.25). pivot_overrun is speed-dependent: retune it whenever
# the pivot cruise changes.

SET twist_hold_gain 8
SET speed_floor 512
SET yaw_taper 800
SET pivot_overrun 2.0

# --- bend 1: half circle, CCW ----------------------------------------
MARK bend1
REPEAT 4
TWIST vx=250 omega=114.6 angle=45 timeout=6
END

# --- bend 2: half circle, CW -----------------------------------------
MARK bend2
REPEAT 4
TWIST vx=250 omega=-114.6 angle=-45 timeout=6
END

# --- bend 3: half circle, CCW ----------------------------------------
MARK bend3
REPEAT 4
TWIST vx=250 omega=114.6 angle=45 timeout=6
END

# --- bend 4: half circle, CW -----------------------------------------
MARK bend4
REPEAT 4
TWIST vx=250 omega=-114.6 angle=-45 timeout=6
END

STOP dwell=0.5

# NOTE ON CLOSURE. A serpentine is an OPEN path: it ends 1000 mm from
# where it started, so the closure number run_tour.py prints is
# meaningless here and should be ignored. What this tour scores is the
# heading at the end -- four half circles alternating direction net to
# ZERO net rotation, so a clean run finishes on the starting heading --
# and the advance (ideal 8r) against the lateral swing (ideal 2r).
